<SiteLayout locale="en" active="execution" title="TypeNN execution model" description="How a Tensor graph runs as TypeShade kernel functions in one fuse scope per data() call.">
  <article class="docs-article">
    <nav class="breadcrumbs" aria-label="Breadcrumb"><a href="/">TypeNN</a><span>/</span><a href="/guide/">Guides</a><span>/</span><span>Execution model</span></nav>
    <header class="page-heading"><p class="eyebrow">Guides</p><h1>Execution model</h1><p class="lead">TypeNN owns the Tensor graph and its meaning. TypeShade owns kernel compilation and program execution.</p></header>
    <section class="doc-section"><h2>Tensor calls build a deferred graph</h2><p>Operations such as <code>matmul()</code>, <code>add()</code>, and <code>relu()</code> do not compute. Each one records a node that holds a TypeShade kernel function, the input Tensors, the scalars the kernel takes by value, and the gradient rule.</p>\1 title="A small Tensor graph" file="model.ts" code={code} /></section>
    <section class="doc-section"><h2>Each data() call runs one fuse scope</h2><p>When <code>data()</code> is requested, TypeNN collects the nodes under the Tensor that have no array yet, makes one resident array for each, and uploads each leaf. It then calls TypeShade <code>fuse</code> with a body that calls each node's kernel function in graph order, and reads the Tensor back once.</p><div class="execution-lanes"><div><span class="lane-label">TypeNN</span><p>Shape checks · graph nodes · autograd · kernel functions</p></div><div><span class="lane-label">TypeShade</span><p>fuse scope plan · dispatches · backend tiers · runtime resources</p></div></div></section>
    <section class="doc-section"><h2>Backends and limits</h2><div class="backend-table" role="table" aria-label="Backend support"><div class="table-row table-head" role="row"><span role="columnheader">Tier</span><span role="columnheader">Selection</span><span role="columnheader">Current note</span></div><div class="table-row" role="row"><strong role="cell">CPU</strong><span role="cell">Auto fallback or explicit</span><span role="cell">Runs the same kernel functions, one call after the other. Suited to correctness and fallback.</span></div><div class="table-row" role="row"><strong role="cell">WebGPU</strong><span role="cell">Auto or explicit</span><span role="cell">Native GPU compute when the browser exposes an adapter. Elementwise chains fuse into one dispatch.</span></div><div class="table-row" role="row"><strong role="cell">WebGL2</strong><span role="cell">Explicit</span><span role="cell">Runs the same kernel functions apart, one call after the other. A call it cannot run goes to the CPU tier.</span></div></div><p class="fine-print">One active TypeNN Device is supported per JavaScript context because TypeShade call-layer configuration is process-global.</p></section>
    <section class="doc-section"><h2>Read the plan in device.lastReport</h2><p>The compiler puts consecutive kernel calls in one dispatch when TypeShade's fusion rules hold. On WebGPU, <code>a.add(b).mul(c).relu()</code> runs as one dispatch. A reduction, a read at another index, or too many storage buffers starts a new dispatch. After a read, <code>device.lastReport</code> shows the tier, the dispatches, the submissions, and the groups of calls. On the CPU tier the report has no dispatches and one group for each call.</p><p><a class="text-link" href="https://github.com/typeshade/typenn#how-a-read-runs">Read how a read runs ↗</a></p></section>
